// bantamkit-mcp — MCP host configuration, five forms.
//
// This file is JSONC for the comments. A real `.mcp.json` (project scope) or the
// `mcpServers` block of a host's user-scope config is plain JSON: copy ONE of the five
// server entries below, drop the comments, and drop the surrounding `_variants` wrapper.
//
// Which one to use is decided by two questions, and both were measured — see
// runtime-ts/README.md for the numbers behind each:
//
//   1. Is `npx` on the PATH the host process actually has?  A GUI-launched host does not
//      source ~/.zshrc or ~/.bash_profile. On the machine this repo was developed on,
//      `env -i PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin sh -c 'command -v npx'` exits 1: node
//      comes from mise, mise is activated from an interactive rc, and `launchctl getenv
//      PATH` is unset. If that is your machine too, form A will fail with ENOENT and you
//      want form B or C.
//
//   2. Does this machine ever start cold and offline?  A cold `npx` needs the registry.
//      Measured: with the registry unreachable, the client sees ZERO bytes on the
//      JSON-RPC channel for 140 s (connection refused) or 590 s (packets dropped) and
//      then the process exits. The host reports a handshake timeout, not a network
//      error. If cold-and-offline is real for you, use form C.
//
// AMENDED 2026-09-15 (job51). Both answers above are now wrong, and form K below is the
// recommended form.
//
//   - Question 2 is not only about a COLD start. A registry spec (`bantamkit-mcp`,
//     `@latest`, `@<version>`) is resolved against the registry on every launch. With a
//     warm cache and the network cut, all three hung silently at the 45 s bound with
//     0 stdout bytes (45.01 s, 45.01 s, 45.02 s). The cold-cache arm, with the registry
//     refusing connections, gave up after 140.29 s with 0 frames. Both are from
//     `node tools/conformance/npx-cold-start.mjs --offline`, 2026-09-15, node v25.2.1,
//     npm 11.6.2, macOS.
//   - Form C does not fix question 1 by itself. See its own amendment.
//
// The answer to both questions is form K: node by absolute path, and a kept install's
// `cli.js` by absolute path.
{
  "_variants": {

    // ------------------------------------------ K. a kept install, no npx (RECOMMENDED)
    // What `npx -y bantamkit-mcp@latest --install <host>` writes from bantamkit-mcp 0.34.0.
    // 0.34.0 was not yet on npm on 2026-09-15; until it is, that command installs 0.33.0,
    // which still writes form A. To make this form by hand today:
    //
    //     npm install --prefix ~/.bantamkit/mcp bantamkit-mcp@0.33.0
    //     node -p process.execPath          # paste the result as "command"
    //
    // A JSON file cannot expand `~`, so both paths are spelled out in full.
    //
    // Measured (npx-cold-start.mjs --offline, kept-install arms; served-tools: dated — the
    // surface was twelve at 0.33.0 and nobody re-ran this offline launch against fourteen):
    // launched this way with PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin and the network cut, it
    // served 12 tools in 0.09 s.
    // The same install's `node_modules/.bin/bantamkit-mcp` exited 127 in 0.16 s with
    // `env: node: No such file or directory`, because its first line is
    // `#!/usr/bin/env node`. That is why "command" is node itself and not the shim.
    //
    // "command" names ONE node version. Under mise it was
    // `~/.local/share/mise/installs/node/25.2.1/bin/node`. Remove or switch away from that
    // version and this entry stops launching. Re-run
    // `npx -y bantamkit-mcp@latest --install <host> --force` to record the node you have
    // now. nvm also keeps each version in its own directory; that was not measured here.
    //
    // Update in place with `npx -y bantamkit-mcp@latest --update` (0.34.0 and later), or
    // `npm install --prefix ~/.bantamkit/mcp bantamkit-mcp@latest`, then reconnect the
    // server in the host. The paths above do not change.
    "K-kept-install": {
      "command": "/Users/you/.local/share/mise/installs/node/25.2.1/bin/node",
      "args": ["/Users/you/.bantamkit/mcp/node_modules/bantamkit-mcp/dist/cli.js"]
    },

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- A. the short form
    // What the package exists for: one line, no clone, no venv, no Python.
    //
    // Requires `npx` on the host's PATH and a network on first run. `-y` is not optional:
    // stdin is the JSON-RPC channel, and an install prompt that read one frame would look
    // like a server that lost a request.
    //
    // AMENDED 2026-09-15: "a network on first run" is false for this spec. It needs the
    // registry on EVERY launch; see the header amendment.
    //
    // `@0.25.0` is a deliberate pin. Without it npx resolves the `latest` dist-tag and
    // caches it, so two machines can run different builds from this identical config and
    // nothing says so. Call `build_identity` and compare `build_id` if you suspect it.
    "A-npx-pinned": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "bantamkit-mcp@0.25.0"]
    },

    // ------------------------------------------------- B. npx, named by absolute path
    // Same thing, for a host that does not inherit your shell's PATH. Find the path with
    // `command -v npx` in your terminal and paste it here verbatim.
    //
    // This still needs the network on a cold cache. It only fixes question 1.
    //
    // AMENDED 2026-09-15: on every launch, not only a cold one (header amendment). Adding
    // `--offline` before `-y` makes npx serve a warm cache without the registry. Measured:
    // 12 tools in 0.40 s. On a cache that has never seen the package it exits 1 in 2.69 s
    // with `ENOTCACHED`. That makes it a middle option for a machine that has already run
    // this spec once, not a way to start on a new one.
    "B-npx-absolute": {
      "command": "/Users/you/.local/share/mise/installs/node/latest/bin/npx",
      "args": ["-y", "bantamkit-mcp@0.25.0"]
    },

    // ------------------------------------------------------- C. installed once, no npx
    //     npm i -g bantamkit-mcp@0.25.0
    //     command -v bantamkit-mcp        # paste the result below
    //
    // Fixes both questions: no PATH lookup of `npx`, no registry on start, and the
    // version is whatever you installed rather than whatever `latest` resolved to today.
    // The cost is that upgrading is now a thing you do on purpose.
    //
    // AMENDED 2026-09-15: "fixes both questions" holds only when `node` itself is on the
    // host's PATH. The command installed here runs `dist/cli.js`, whose first line is
    // `#!/usr/bin/env node`. The kept install's `.bin` shim is that same file, and under
    // PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin it exited 127 (form K's note). A global bin was
    // not launched separately here. Where node is not on the host's PATH, use form K's
    // shape instead: node by absolute path, then the global install's `dist/cli.js`.
    "C-global-install": {
      "command": "/usr/local/bin/bantamkit-mcp",
      "args": []
    },

    // ------------------------------------------------------ D. a checkout, for developers
    // `npx` will happily serve the PUBLISHED build while you believe you are testing your
    // worktree — the sh launcher's "code from this checkout" guarantee has no npx
    // analogue. Point at the built entry point by absolute path instead.
    //
    //     cd <worktree>/runtime-ts && npm run build
    //
    // BANTAMKIT_ASSETS is optional: without it the packaged arm finds
    // <worktree>/assets via the repo-root fallback. Set it when you want to be certain
    // which pack answered, and confirm with `build_identity`.assets_root.
    "D-worktree": {
      "command": "/absolute/path/to/node",
      "args": ["/absolute/path/to/worktree/runtime-ts/dist/cli.js"],
      "env": {
        "BANTAMKIT_ASSETS": "/absolute/path/to/worktree/assets"
      }
    }
  },

  // ------------------------------------------------------------------------- arguments
  //
  // PASS NO ARGUMENTS unless you know why. With an empty `args` (beyond the npx spec, or
  // the `cli.js` path in forms K and D) the
  // server binds `Memory.layered` — the project store found from the working directory,
  // the profile layer, and any `extra_stores` from `.bantamkit/config.yaml` — and every
  // recall line carries its layer tag, `[project] ` / `[extra:<name>] ` / `[profile] `.
  //
  //   --store <path>          bind ONE store; drops the layer tag. A debugging flag.
  //   --start <path>          where the project-store search begins, instead of cwd.
  //   --k <n>                 recall budget, default 3, clamped to the advertised 5.
  //   --index-budget <bytes>  default 24000; the index refuses to grow past it.
  //   --assets-root           print the resolved asset pack and exit. Never a frame.
  //
  // BANTAMKIT_MEMORY_DIR overrides the project store for every layer; it is read by both
  // runtimes and is the one knob that answers for the whole machine.
  "_notes": "see runtime-ts/README.md for the measured numbers behind every claim here"
}
